@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

For conditionals, we accept anything to be true that is not the explicit
@code{false} object.

@example rust
/// is_true holds for every value except the false object.
fn is_true(v: &Value) -> bool {
    !matches!(v, Value::Bool(false))
}
@end example

@subsubheading Representing procedures

To handle primitives, we assume that we have available the following
procedures:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{apply_procedure ⟨@var{proc}⟩ ⟨@var{args}⟩}

@noindent
applies the given primitive procedure to the argument values in the list
@code{⟨}@var{args}@code{⟩} and returns the result of the application.

@item
@code{matches!(⟨@var{proc}⟩, Value::Primitive @{ .. @})}

@noindent
tests whether @code{⟨}@var{proc}@code{⟩} is a primitive procedure.

@end itemize

@noindent
These mechanisms for handling primitives are further described in
@ref{4.1.4}.

Compound procedures are constructed from parameters, procedure bodies, and
environments using the constructor @code{make_procedure}, which packages them
into a @code{Closure} value. A procedure made by a definition carries its
definition name, which the printer prints as
@code{#[compound-procedure name]}.

@example rust
fn make_named_procedure(
    &self,
    name: &str,
    params: &[Symbol],
    rest: Option<&Symbol>,
    body: &[Value],
    env: &Rc<Env>,
) -> EvalResult {
    Ok(Value::Closure(Rc::new(Closure {
        name: Some(Rc::from(name)),
        params: params.to_vec(),
        rest: rest.cloned(),
        body: body.to_vec(),
        env: Rc::clone(env),
    })))
}
@end example

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
We use the following operations for manipulating environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{lookup_variable_value ⟨@var{var}⟩ ⟨@var{env}⟩}

returns the value that is bound to the symbol @code{⟨}@var{var}@code{⟩} in the
environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}, or signals an error if the variable is
unbound.

@item
@code{extend_environment ⟨@var{vars}⟩ ⟨@var{values}⟩ ⟨@var{base-env}⟩}

returns a new environment, consisting of a new frame in which the symbols in
the list @code{⟨}@var{vars}@code{⟩} are bound to the corresponding elements in
the list @code{⟨}@var{values}@code{⟩}, where the enclosing environment is the
environment @code{⟨}@var{base-env}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{define_variable_ ⟨@var{var}⟩ ⟨@var{value}⟩ ⟨@var{env}⟩}

adds to the first frame in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩} a new
binding that associates the variable @code{⟨}@var{var}@code{⟩} with the value
@code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{set_variable_value_ ⟨@var{var}⟩ ⟨@var{value}⟩ ⟨@var{env}⟩}

changes the binding of the variable @code{⟨}@var{var}@code{⟩} in the
environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩} so that the variable is now bound to the
value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}, or signals an error if the variable is
unbound.

@end itemize

@noindent
To implement these operations we use the environments of @ref{3.2}: an
environment is a chain of frames, and the enclosing environment of an
environment is the rest of the chain.  Each frame is a mutable table from names
to values, searched newest first, and the four operations are thin wrappers
over that representation.

@example rust
fn lookup_variable_value(name: &str, env: &Rc<Env>) -> EvalResult {
    env.lookup(name)
}

fn extend_environment(
    procedure: &str,
    params: &[Symbol],
    rest: Option<&Symbol>,
    args: &[Value],
    base: &Rc<Env>,
) -> Result<Rc<Env>, SchemeError> {
    // ... an arity error when the counts disagree, one frame
    // binding each parameter to its argument in front of base ...
    let frame = Env::child(base);
    for (param, arg) in params.iter().zip(args) {
        frame.define(param.clone(), arg.clone());
    }
    Ok(frame)
}

fn set_variable_value_(name: &str, value: Value, env: &Rc<Env>) -> Result<(), SchemeError> {
    env.set(name, value)
}

fn define_variable_(name: &str, value: Value, env: &Rc<Env>) -> EvalResult {
    env.define(Rc::from(name), value);
    Ok(Value::sym("ok"))
}
@end example

@noindent
To extend an environment by a new frame that associates variables with values,
we make a frame binding each variable to the value at the same position, and we
adjoin this to the environment.  We signal an error if the number of variables
does not match the number of values.

@noindent
To look up a variable in an environment, we search the table of the first
frame.  If we find the desired variable, we return its value.  If we do not
find the variable in the current frame, we search the enclosing environment, and
so on.  If no frame binds the variable, we signal an ``unbound variable''
error.

@noindent
To set a variable to a new value in a specified environment, we search for the
variable, just as in @code{lookup_variable_value}, and change the corresponding
value when we find it.

@noindent
To define a variable, we search the first frame for a binding for the variable,
and change the binding if it exists (just as in @code{set_variable_value_}).
If no such binding exists, we adjoin one to the first frame.

@noindent
The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  In a
production-quality Lisp system, the speed of the evaluator's environment
operations---especially that of variable lookup---has a major impact on the
performance of the system.  The representation described here, although
conceptually simple, is not efficient and would not ordinarily be used in a
production system.@footnote{The drawback of this representation (as well as the
variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is that the evaluator may have to search
through many frames to find the binding for a given variable.  (Such
an approach is referred to as @newterm{deep binding}.)  One way to avoid this
inefficiency is to make use of a strategy called @newterm{lexical addressing},
which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

@quotation
