@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in Rust) of
the process by which Lisp expressions are evaluated.  One advantage of
expressing the evaluator as a program is that we can run the program.  This
gives us, running within Rust, a working model of how Lisp evaluates
expressions.  This can serve as a framework for experimenting with evaluation
rules, as we shall do later in this chapter.

Our evaluator program reduces expressions ultimately to the application of
primitive procedures.  Therefore, all that we need to run the evaluator is to
create a mechanism that calls on the Rust runtime to model the application of
primitive procedures.

There must be a binding for each primitive procedure name, so that when
@code{eval} evaluates the operator of an application of a primitive, it will
find an object to pass to @code{apply}.  We thus set up a global environment
that associates unique objects with the names of the primitive procedures that
can appear in the expressions we will be evaluating.  The global environment
also includes bindings for the symbols @code{true} and @code{false}, so that
they can be used as variables in expressions to be evaluated.

@example rust
fn setup_environment_in(sink: &OutputSink) -> Rc<Env> {
    let env = Env::global();
    env.define(Rc::from("true"), Value::boolean(true));
    env.define(Rc::from("false"), Value::boolean(false));
    for (name, handler) in primitive_table(sink) {
        env.define(Rc::from(name), Value::Primitive {
            name: Rc::from(name),
            f: handler,
        });
    }
    env
}
@end example

@noindent
It does not matter how we represent the primitive procedure objects, so long as
@code{apply_procedure} can identify and apply them.  We have chosen to represent
a primitive procedure as a value carrying its name and a Rust handler that
implements that primitive.

@example rust
/// One entry of the primitive table, as installed:
/// ("car", the handler applied by apply_procedure)
@end example

@noindent
@code{setup_environment} will get the primitive names and implementation
functions from a table:@footnote{Any function written in Rust can be used as a
primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  The name of a primitive installed in
the evaluator need not be the same as the name of its implementation; the names
are the same here because the metacircular evaluator implements Scheme itself.
Thus, for example, we could put an entry named @code{first} for the function of
@code{car}, or an entry named @code{square} for a squaring function, in the
primitive table.}

@example rust
let table = core_table();
// car, cdr, cons, list, null?, pair?, eq?, equal?, +, -, *, /,
// =, <, >, <=, >=, remainder, quotient, abs, not, display,
// newline, error, number?, symbol?, string?, apply
@end example

@noindent
To apply a primitive procedure, we simply apply the handler the value carries
to the arguments, an ordinary Rust call.@footnote{The metacircular evaluator's
@code{apply_procedure} (@ref{4.1.1}) models the working of the host's
application.  In Rust the two never share a name, so defining the
metacircular @code{apply_procedure} masks nothing: its primitive arm calls the
handler the value carries.  The object language's @code{apply} and @code{map}
are applied by @code{apply_procedure} itself, because they must put procedure
@emph{values} into the operator position, which a bare handler cannot do.}

@noindent
For convenience in running the metacircular evaluator, we provide a
@newterm{driver loop} that models the read-eval-print loop of a Lisp system.
It prints a @newterm{prompt}, reads an input expression, evaluates this
expression in the global environment, and prints the result.  We precede each
printed result by an @newterm{output prompt} so as to distinguish the value of
the expression from other output that may be printed.@footnote{The shared
reader takes the input the user types and returns the next complete expression
as a form.  For example, if the user types @code{(+ 23 x)}, the reader returns
an application whose operator is the symbol @code{+} and whose operands are the
number 23 and the symbol @code{x}.  If the user types @code{'x}, the reader
returns the quotation of the symbol @code{x}.}

@example rust
fn driver_transcript(lines: &[&str]) -> String {
    // For each input line: read it, evaluate it in the global
    // environment, and announce the value behind the output prompt.
    // A raised error ends the transcript with one Error line.
}
@end example

@noindent
We use the shared printer to avoid printing the environment part of a compound
procedure, which may be a very long list (or may even contain cycles).

@example rust
print_value(&the_named_procedure_f);
// => #[compound-procedure f]
@end example

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to create the global environment
and run forms in it.  Here is a sample interaction:

@example rust
run_program("(define (append x y) \
             (if (null? x) y (cons (car x) (append (cdr x) y))))");
// => ok
run_program("(append '(a b c) '(d e f))");
// => (a b c d e f)
@end example

@quotation
